<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alcaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 625–c. 580 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alcaeus of Mytilene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alcaeus was a Greek lyric poet of Mytilene on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a>. An aristocrat, he took part in the city's factional struggles against the tyrants Melanchrus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrsilus">Myrsilus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus</a> and spent years in exile. His poems on politics, war and drinking survive in fragments, and the Alcaic stanza is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcaeus.jpg" alt="Alcaeus" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brygos_Painter_ARV_385_228_Alkaios_and_Sappho_-_Dionysos_and_maenad_(01).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
